  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 152 PM MDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nFriday's high temperatures will be 5 to 20 degrees below normal\nareawide following the backdoor front. Winds will slowly decrease\nduring the morning hours, though breezy conditions will persist\nthrough the rest of the day. \n\nA widespread freeze is expected Friday night under excellent\nradiational cooling conditions. For those areas that will freeze\ntonight and/or Thursday night, a hard freeze is likely Friday \nnight. Though the ABQ Sunport will likely stay above freezing,\nareas around KAEG and Belen may see sub-freezing temperatures.\nKROW, KTCC, Socorro and KFMN will be close to freezing, but could\nstay just above.  \n\nWhile the upper low mentioned above shifted toward the northern\nGreat Plains, a baggy trough that was left behind closed into a \nlow and retrograded to the west of northern Baja California. Over \nthe weekend and into Monday, this feature will weaken and \ngradually shift northeastward. A few showers or thunderstorms may \ndevelop across western NM each day but coverage will be spotty at \nbest. Temperatures will gradually rise back toward normal by \nMonday.\n\nAll eyes will then be on the next upper low that dives southward\nalong the west coast Tuesday. Depending on it's position, moisture\nmay increase with enhanced southerly flow, or if the low is too\nfar west, weak ridging may dominate NM.",
